What is still missing (important, but fixable)
1. Sample size / unit-of-analysis clarity ⚠️ (still the biggest risk)
What’s improved

You’ve clarified scenarios and runs more clearly.

What’s still missing

It is still not unambiguously clear whether:

The reported distributions are based on:

Per-operation timings (thousands/millions of points), or

Per-run aggregates (hundreds of points)

An examiner will ask:

“What exactly is one data point in these distributions?”

Why this matters

Without crystal clarity:

Claims about distribution shape and stability are vulnerable

Statistical power is ambiguous

Minimal fix (recommended)

Add one explicit sentence early in Chapter 4, e.g.:

“Each data point represents the latency of a single cryptographic operation invocation, aggregated across n runs per scenario, yielding approximately X total measurements per algorithm.”

or, if aggregated:

“Each data point represents the median latency per run, yielding n observations per scenario.”

This is a high-leverage fix.

2. Tail latency interpretation is still underdeveloped ⚠️

You report p95 / p99 — good.
But interpretation still focuses primarily on means and medians.

An examiner in cybersecurity + real-time systems may ask:

“Does PQC increase jitter or tail amplification even if the mean impact is small?”

Minimal fix

Add one short paragraph explicitly stating one of the following:

Either:

“No systematic tail amplification was observed across PQC variants.”

Or:

“While tail latencies increased slightly, the absolute deltas remained within operational tolerance.”

You do not need more plots — just interpretation.

3. Environment confounding: now implied, not explicit ⚠️

You implicitly handle environment noise well, but you no longer explicitly say:

“Environment effects were treated as constant within each experimental run.”

This was clearer in earlier drafts.

Minimal fix

Add one sentence in Chapter 4 methodology recap:

“All comparative analyses were conducted within identical execution environments, ensuring that observed differences are attributable to cryptographic algorithm choice rather than deployment context.”

This pre-empts a very common examiner question.